  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 PM Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Seasonable early-week temperatures will trend above \nnormal for Wed through Fri. \n\nAs a frontal zone progresses further to our S and SW, surface high \npressure centered over the Great Lakes this midday will build \nsouthward into central NC as its center slides eastward. Dry air and \nnear-normal thicknesses will result in seasonable temps and low \nhumidity through Tue. As deep mid-upper level troughing along the \nEastern Seaboard shifts eastward, renewed mid level ridging will \nbuild eastward from the Plains into our area starting mid week. \nSlightly above-normal low level thicknesses Wed will climb further \nand peak Thu/Fri with readings around 20 m above normal, supporting \nhighs around 2 categories above normal, mainly around 90 to the low \n90s. Heat index values will top out in the 90s to around 100 both \ndays. The cold front approaching Thu night and accompanying clouds \nshould curb the heat a bit in the NW CWA Fri and areawide Sat into \nSun. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Dry weather through mid week yields to increasing \nconvection chances Fri and Sat, perhaps lasting into Sun. \n\nSurface high pressure centered to our N will continue drifting E and \nSE over the Mid Atlantic region and off the coast through Wed, \nkeeping us within deeply dry and stable air, with low PWs and no \npops. While temps will start to warm back up to above normal Wed, \nthe 850 mb ridging stretching from OK/TX east to off the Southeast \ncoast will limit significant Gulf/Atlantic moisture influx, keeping \nus largely free of rain chances through Thu, although our southeast \nareas could see a few late-day sea-breeze storms Thu. The mid level \nridge will then break down by Wed night as broad northern stream \ntroughing digs over the Upper Midwest and Great Lakes, with its \naccompanying surface cold front approaching our area from the NW \nlate Thu, resulting in increasing moisture flux and a rebound to \nnear or above normal PW by Fri. With the increasing mid level flow \nhelping to boost deep layer bulk shear and increasing low level \nmoisture leading to increased daytime CAPE, the confidence in the \nchance for afternoon and evening showers and storms for Fri and Sat \nis also increasing. With the dip in the westerlies reaching down \ncloser to our latitude and resulting in passing of multiple weak \nperturbations over NC, these higher convection chances may linger \ninto Sun, particularly over our S and E, if the surface front slows \nor stalls over our region.",
